==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|exclame}} {{defdate|16th|17th c.}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*kelh₁-}}
From {{der|en|frm|exclamer}}, from {{der|en|la|exclamo|exclāmō, exclāmāre|call out}}, from {{m|la|ex-}} + {{m|la|clāmō||to call}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɛkˈskleɪm/|/ɪkˈskleɪm/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-exclaim.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪm|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[cry out]] suddenly, from some strong emotion.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-1|V|3|text=I am a soldier, and unapt to weep,<br>Or to '''exclaim''' on fortune’s fickleness.}}
#* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones|1|1|9|page=33|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004794856.0001.001|text=Very grave and good Women '''exclaimed''' against Men who begot Children and then disowned them.}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Emma|12|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/158/158-h/158-h.htm|text=This wretched note was the finale of Emma’s breakfast. When once it had been read, there was no doing any thing, but lament and '''exclaim'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1925|author=w:Virginia Woolf|title=w:Mrs Dalloway|location=New York|publisher=Harcourt Brace Jovanovich|year_published=1985|page=114|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/mrsdalloway000wool
|passage={{...}} he could remember Sally tearing off a rose, stopping to '''exclaim''' at the beauty of the cabbage leaves in the moonlight {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author=w:Alan Hollinghurst|title={{w|The Stranger's Child|The Stranger’s Child}}|location=New York|publisher=Knopf|section=Part 4, Chapter 1, p. 285|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=HLhnPVspmxgC&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false
|passage={{...}} at the front door below a few guests were leaving, and the bright rectangle widened and narrowed as they slipped out into the night, laughing and '''exclaiming''' about the weather.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To say suddenly and with strong emotion.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1603|author=w:Michael Drayton|title=The Barrons Wars in the Raigne of {{w|Edward II of England|Edward the Second}}|location=London|publisher=N. Ling|chapter=Alice Countesse of Salisburie, to the blacke Prince|page=31|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A20811.0001.001
|passage=Must she be forc’d, t’'''exclaime''' th’iniurious wrong?<br>Offred by him, whom she hath lou’d so long?<br>Nay, I will tell, and I durst almost sweare,<br>''Edward'' will blush, when he his fault shall heare.}}
#* {{RQ:Smollett Roderick Random|volume=2|chapter=40|page=28|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004833141.0001.002|text={{...}} her aunt, after having stared at me a good while with a look of amazement, '''exclaimed''', “In the name of heaven! Who art thou?”—}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Nicholas Nickleby|12|text=Without returning any direct reply, Miss Squeers, all at once, fell into a paroxysm of spiteful tears, and '''exclaimed''' that she was a wretched, neglected, miserable castaway.}}
#* {{RQ:Chambers Younger Set|IX|passage=“Heavens!” '''exclaimed''' Nina, “the blue-stocking and the fogy!—and yours ''are'' pale blue, Eileen!—you’re about as self-conscious as Drina—slumping there with your hair tumbling ''à la'' Mérode! Oh, it's very picturesque, of course, but a straight spine and good grooming is better.{{nb...}}”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2017|author=w:André Aciman|title=Enigma Variations|location=New York|publisher=Farrar, Straus and Giroux|chapter=Manfred|page=135|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=xBFeDAAAQBAJ&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false
|passage=You never pump your arm when you score, you never '''exclaim''' anything, you don’t even smile when you fire a perfect backhand straight down the line.}}

